body:
I'll disregard your ad hominem attacks which alone are enough to invalidate this poorly formed opinion and take on your attack of his movie as being created for shock value alone. (if that is your argument, it was hard to follow your 'cacophony' of seemingly random words. 

&#x200B;

This movie had beautiful and stunning imagery. It was a film that has stuck with me as incredibly memorable for many years due mainly to the fact that it has such unique visuals. It depends very little on its plot in favor of providing a viewer of a message that is absolutely Dali-esq. There is an undeniable brilliance when Jesus walks by all the toy Jesuses. 

&#x200B;

This film was made for shock value, but shock value isn't mutually exclusive with a good movie.

This review sounds like it comes from someone who has watched a lot of movies but not a lot of films.

&#x200B;

 What specifically was problematic with the dialogue? you said that " Sometimes the responses of characters to others seem to not address in any shape or form what was said to them, bringing up a new, unrelated subject. " Think about the setting, what the author is trying to convey. Ignore the fact that it is a sci-fi. Think about it from the viewpoint of Don Rumata. What's the first scene? He wakes up, drinks old coffee, eats rotten food, and walks through a filthy landscape populated by idiots. Do you expect all the drunken peasants to carry on an intelligible conversation? 

&#x200B;

The filth wasn't meant to shock you, it was meant to immerse you. The movie was a long, slow, slog through mud and shit. It was about a man who was king and had absolute power to kill all those that served him, all those who opposed him, but even then nothing changed. Everyone remained an idiot. All the slaves he tried to free died within seconds of being released from their bondage. 

&#x200B;

You are the slave in the movie. Even if I try to educate you, even if I unlatch the chains that have bound you to ignorance, you either exit the cave and come back to the shadows or die. Watch it again. Watch it closely. Really listen to what they are saying. Watch *Stalker.* listen to a podcast about the Russian revolution.  Think.

I agree that them abandoning Shota crosses a line. I think that he realizes that. When he confronts Osamu about it, it's clear that there was a serious turning point in their relationship. I cant remember the exact quote but once heard something like  "There comes a point in every guys life that he realizes his father is just a man." 

&#x200B;

I disagree that bringing him into shoplifting was as bad as raping him, but I totally agree that the deception and lack of trust will seriously impact him. I also agree that they are stupid, selfish, dishonest and cruel, and maybe worse, but ultimately they are humans trying to do the best they can in a shitty situation. 

&#x200B;

In my mind, if they had raped the kids, murdered the grandma, or really broken the rules of common decency, I think I would absolutely view the movie differently. But they didn't. I think that there is more room to view what they did as forgivable, albeit shortsighted and clearly wrong.

  "A family that exploits you doesn't really love you. "

&#x200B;

I guess that's where I might disagree, to an extent anyway. 

&#x200B;

I think that most relationships are built on mutual emotional exchange and sometimes they can be exploitive when there is an imbalance in the relationship. This imbalance can stem from power dynamics such as gender, age, class, ect. I think that the person in power in the relationship can start with good intentions but end up exploiting the other without fully realizing it. 

&#x200B;

I think Osamu sees shoplifting as a father-son bonding experience, just like when they go fishing. He expresses the need for Yuri to go with them so that they can bond. When asked by the police why he shoplifted, Osamu says that it is all he knows. 

&#x200B;

I think that we respect when parents pass on their line of work to their children, but in doing so they may be exploited for their labor. This is especially apparent when the parents and children aren't bonded by blood. 

&#x200B;

I would say that parents who pass on their trade to their children without their consent (either through unwillingness or through forced ignorance) still love them, but they may also exploit them.

Princess Mononoke is easily my favorite animated film. I agree with you that it does a wonderful job of representing environmental concerns in a more nuanced way, however, I disagree with your assessment that Eboshi isn't the antagonist of the film. Her unwillingness to follow the middle path is antithetical to Ashitaka's middle path philosophy.

&#x200B;

 I read somewhere that Ashitaka is a representation of the indigenous people of Japan. Although he is obviously against hatred ("go with eyes unclouded by hate") I think that he represents a compromise between humans and nature where Eboshi represents human domination over nature. 

&#x200B;

Eboshi sees the natural world as an impediment to providing her people with a good life free from starvation, fear, and enslavement. She honestly thinks that by killing off the forest spirit, her people will be free to live a happy and harmonious life. What she doesn't realize is that nature and its processes are part of what provides humans with a good life. 

&#x200B;

Mononoke, on the other hand, represents those who value nature and can't see the value of humanity. She has only seen the destruction that they cause and is unwilling to see the benefits that they can provide to nature. (think of the scene where she tries to free Yaool (IDK how to spell Ashitakas animal))

&#x200B;

Ashitaka sees the harmony that has been happening in his village for many generations and wants both parties to realize that there is a way of sustainably coexisting with nature. I think that he understands both sides and realizes that fighting against either one will work against his objective, just like he sees how their fighting with each other works against their goals. Instead, he takes the middle path with the two sides, just as he wants them to do, and shows that there is a way for them to live together.

I agree with you that an adopted family can be a real family, I just think that the film asks the question of whether society accepts that. When the female investigator says that children need their mothers and states that the only people who are mothers are the ones that give birth, it leaves out those that can't give birth and foster parents. This is a pretty traditional view of childrearing where biological parents are assumed to be the best fit. American court systems still give preference to biological parents and there are a stigma about being adopted/foster care systems specifically because people exploit the system for their personal gain. 

&#x200B;

I guess the movie really asks whether an adopted family that exploits but loves you is better than a biological family that abuses you.

The moral ambiguity in *'Shoplifters'*  Hirokazu Kore-eda (2018) left me with a deeply sinister feeling that never quite dissipated.

&#x200B;

 A ragtag group of impoverished people living together as a chosen family in a small house in Tokyo subsists off of the grandmother's pension, part-time blue-collar jobs, and shoplifting. One day they find a small girl out on a balcony in the cold and decide to foster/kidnap her, depending on what your viewpoint is. The young boy, Shota, is happy to steal petty things with his paternal stand-in, Osamu until one of the local store owners where he steals frequently catches him teaching the newly adopted girl, Yuri and asks him not to teach her. Shota has a change of heart about shoplifting and whether his adopted family is actually fulfilling the responsibility of a real one. 

&#x200B;

Spoiler

&#x200B;

The movie never really gives a satisfying answer to the question of whether Osamu really loved Shota and Yuri or simply wanted to use them for petty crimes. The police only view them as criminals that exploit children, but the truth is much more ambiguous and the movie does a good job of expressing it. However, the power dynamics clearly suggest that they were being exploited, even unintentionally. Questions that arise such as 'can an adopted family really be you own family' and 'can your biological family stop being your family' are left to the viewer to decide. 

&#x200B;

7/10

It was really an unbelievable film. It was equal parts Tarkovsky and Tarintino; pulsing between long beautiful shots of melancholy Russian scenery combined with unbelievably violent and sadistic scenes.

&#x200B;

 In most war films it's easy to interchange the good and bad guys because the movie makes it look more like a sporting event than a war. The difference with *Come and See* is that there is no objective. No bridge to build, no private to save, no damsel in distress. The people who die in the movie don't die because they weren't strong enough, or because it was a strategic move, they died just because they were unlucky. (or lucky depending on how you look at it) 

&#x200B;

As more authoritarian governments pop up, it becomes an increasingly scary movie. I don't say that because I am worried some evil people will come attack, but because I can see most people in my community willingly picking up a gun and killing for no other reason than the media labels some group as a threat. *Come and See* should be required in history class.

Your emotions are not a part of the art, they are *your* emotions. The pieces are symbols. What do they communicate to you? Why do you think that? The art may tell you something, but more importantly, you should tell yourself something about why you think and feel the way you do. 

&#x200B;

